WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.993
Real data is never exact

2
00:00:04.993 --> 00:00:07.003
measurements are noisy

3
00:00:07.003 --> 00:00:09.850
and computers round every number.

4
00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:11.860
Here are two equations.

5
00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.125
The first line is x plus y equals two.

6
00:00:15.125 --> 00:00:18.140
The second is almost the same line.

7
00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:20.420
They cross at one, one.

8
00:00:20.504 --> 00:00:23.021
Now change only the last digit

9
00:00:23.105 --> 00:00:25.035
of the second equation

10
00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:27.469
from two point zero zero one

11
00:00:27.553 --> 00:00:29.818
to two point zero zero two.

12
00:00:29.818 --> 00:00:32.503
How far does the crossing move?

13
00:00:32.503 --> 00:00:33.930
Pause and guess.

14
00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:39.515
To answer, we need sizes.

15
00:00:39.595 --> 00:00:41.520
The ordinary length of a

16
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.445
vector is its two norm.

17
00:00:43.445 --> 00:00:46.974
The one norm adds the sizes of the entries

18
00:00:46.974 --> 00:00:49.700
like walking along a street grid.

19
00:00:49.781 --> 00:00:52.026
The infinity norm keeps only

20
00:00:52.106 --> 00:00:53.550
the largest entry.

21
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.393
All vectors of length one

22
00:00:56.393 --> 00:00:58.982
a circle for the two norm

23
00:00:58.982 --> 00:01:01.666
a diamond for the one norm

24
00:01:01.666 --> 00:01:05.118
and a square for the infinity norm.

25
00:01:05.118 --> 00:01:08.090
For three, minus four, twelve

26
00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:10.967
the three sizes are nineteen

27
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:12.980
thirteen and twelve.

28
00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:14.838
In machine learning

29
00:01:14.913 --> 00:01:17.401
the one norm and the two norm are

30
00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:19.814
the usual penalties on weights

31
00:01:19.814 --> 00:01:21.548
in lasso and in ridge.

32
00:01:21.548 --> 00:01:22.528
From now on

33
00:01:22.528 --> 00:01:25.770
a norm without a label means the two norm.

34
00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.678
The size of a matrix is how

35
00:01:28.764 --> 00:01:30.488
much it can stretch.

36
00:01:30.574 --> 00:01:34.022
Our matrix A from the SVD lectures turns

37
00:01:34.108 --> 00:01:37.039
the unit circle into this ellipse.

38
00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:40.229
Its two norm is the longest stretch

39
00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:42.470
sigma max, which is four.

40
00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.904
The Frobenius norm treats the matrix

41
00:01:45.989 --> 00:01:49.758
as one long list of numbers: root seventeen

42
00:01:49.758 --> 00:01:51.985
about four point one two.

43
00:01:51.985 --> 00:01:55.583
And the inverse undoes the shortest axis

44
00:01:55.583 --> 00:01:59.180
its norm is one over sigma min, here one.

45
00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.388
Solve A x equals b, then nudge b a little.

46
00:02:03.475 --> 00:02:07.016
The relative change in x is at most kappa

47
00:02:07.103 --> 00:02:09.781
times the relative change in b.

48
00:02:09.781 --> 00:02:12.286
Kappa, the condition number

49
00:02:12.286 --> 00:02:14.791
is sigma max over sigma min

50
00:02:14.791 --> 00:02:17.901
the longest axis over the shortest.

51
00:02:17.901 --> 00:02:18.938
The reason

52
00:02:19.024 --> 00:02:22.220
the change in x is at most the change

53
00:02:22.307 --> 00:02:24.553
in b divided by sigma min

54
00:02:24.553 --> 00:02:28.440
while b itself is at most sigma max times x.

55
00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:32.287
For our ellipse, kappa is four over one

56
00:02:32.287 --> 00:02:32.812
four.

57
00:02:32.812 --> 00:02:35.872
A rotation keeps the circle round

58
00:02:35.872 --> 00:02:39.369
every stretch is one, and kappa is one

59
00:02:39.369 --> 00:02:41.030
the best possible.

60
00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.363
Back to the two lines.

61
00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:46.550
Their matrix M has singular values two

62
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.621
and five ten-thousandths.

63
00:02:48.621 --> 00:02:51.250
So kappa is about four thousand.

64
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.251
Change the last digit.

65
00:02:53.251 --> 00:02:55.802
The second line shifts by a hair

66
00:02:55.802 --> 00:02:59.028
but because the lines are almost parallel

67
00:02:59.028 --> 00:03:02.105
the crossing slides all the way to zero

68
00:03:02.105 --> 00:03:02.605
two.

69
00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:05.257
The data changed by about three

70
00:03:05.335 --> 00:03:07.214
hundredths of a percent.

71
00:03:07.214 --> 00:03:10.580
The answer changed by one hundred percent

72
00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:14.103
about two thousand eight hundred times more

73
00:03:14.103 --> 00:03:15.590
still under kappa.

74
00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.820
The worst direction is the short

75
00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:20.710
axis of the ellipse.

76
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.050
A change of b along it is

77
00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:27.100
divided by sigma min, and sigma min is tiny.

78
00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.120
A common shortcut

79
00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:32.320
the determinant of M is one thousandth

80
00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.880
so M must be badly conditioned.

81
00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.440
Test it on zero point zero zero

82
00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:39.360
one times the identity.

83
00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.000
Its determinant is even smaller

84
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.200
one millionth.

85
00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:46.129
But it shrinks the circle evenly

86
00:03:46.129 --> 00:03:47.926
into a smaller circle.

87
00:03:48.004 --> 00:03:49.958
Every error is multiplied

88
00:03:50.036 --> 00:03:51.677
by the same thousand

89
00:03:51.677 --> 00:03:54.490
the relative error does not change

90
00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:55.897
and kappa is one.

91
00:03:55.897 --> 00:03:58.710
Only the ratio of stretches counts.

92
00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:01.892
What does kappa mean in practice?

93
00:04:01.971 --> 00:04:04.336
A computer keeps about sixteen

94
00:04:04.415 --> 00:04:05.913
significant digits.

95
00:04:05.913 --> 00:04:08.752
Its rounding step, machine epsilon

96
00:04:08.752 --> 00:04:12.300
is about two times ten to the minus sixteen.

97
00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:13.640
A rule of thumb

98
00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:16.242
a condition number of ten to the

99
00:04:16.321 --> 00:04:18.450
k can cost about k of them.

100
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.632
For M, kappa is about four thousand

101
00:04:21.632 --> 00:04:24.700
between ten cubed and ten to the four

102
00:04:24.779 --> 00:04:26.981
we lose three or four digits

103
00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.791
and keep about twelve.

104
00:04:28.791 --> 00:04:31.623
With kappa near ten to the sixteen

105
00:04:31.623 --> 00:04:32.960
nothing is left.

106
00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:36.269
So the norm of a matrix is the

107
00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.907
longest axis of its ellipse

108
00:04:38.998 --> 00:04:42.364
and the condition number compares the

109
00:04:42.455 --> 00:04:45.275
longest axis with the shortest.

110
00:04:45.366 --> 00:04:48.458
It tells how much a small error in

111
00:04:48.549 --> 00:04:51.460
the data can grow in the answer.

112
00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:52.617
Next part

113
00:04:52.617 --> 00:04:56.248
why good software never forms A transpose A

114
00:04:56.328 --> 00:04:58.830
and what conditioning means for

115
00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:01.250
regression and low precision.
